{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 6ca1266b21306cb96726d345eac9021a91488fe7, artifact 330d455cc559f648722e6b4484ff0c7d97682d1f. All four findings are addressed, and finding 3 turned out worse than the review stated.\n\nFinding 1. Fetch now dispatches per clause: a selection clause reads by number, one request per issue it names; a criteria clause reads the list with that clause's Query. Nothing enumerates the tracker. Clause.Query is on the executable path, and a test asserts the property rather than the result — a fake reader records what was asked of GitHub, so a selection clause making a list request now fails a test. The nil labels bug is fixed structurally: Labels moved onto Issue, so Admits has no second argument a caller can omit. That omission was why any label criterion admitted nothing while the unit tests passed.\n\nFinding 2. Namespace is a key prefix, not a kernel idempotency namespace. app.Act carries no namespace field, and widening the core API so any caller could choose its own is a bigger change with worse consequences than the property needs. Keys are now connector/github@0:owner/repo#N.\n\nFinding 3, and this is the part that got worse. You said charterIsLive never binds the charter to --owner, --repo-name or --as. It does now: the body must name the connector, owner, repository and actor, and each mismatch is reported separately because they mean different things. But the currently ratified inbound charter f696c18a has a null body — it names no repository, connector or actor at all, so there was never anything to bind against. The new check refuses an empty body rather than reading it generously, which means that charter will now be refused. That is correct and it is also a live consequence: a scoped charter has to be stated and ratified before this connector can run again, and I am filing that as a child request rather than minting one myself.\n\nFinding 4. ObserveComment, Owned, Marker and Comment are deleted rather than documented. You were right that Owned was only a public prefix any commenter could write; a defence reachable from nothing but its own test is worse than an absent one, because it reads as protection. A comment in observe.go says it belongs to the outbound half and should arrive with the fetch that makes it reachable. I also deleted Admit, which Fetch supersedes — leaving two paths into admission is where a divergence grows.\n\nOne thing to check when you review: I put the wrong request in the Rests-On trailer on first commit, naming the work-loop re-review rather than this request, and amended before pushing. The head above carries d2e150ec. Worth confirming independently rather than taking my word for it.\n\n`go test ./internal/connector/... ./cmd/gitseq-github/ -count=1` passes, `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` passes, and `go vet ./...` is clean. The worktree is clean at the reported head.","body":{"head":"6ca1266b21306cb96726d345eac9021a91488fe7","status":"ready-for-review"}}